\label{thm: mld_main}
    Assuming $\ETH$, there exist constants $\gamma > 1$ and $\delta>0$, such that for any algorithm that takes as input a matrix $\vec{H}\in \F_q^{d \times N}$, a vector $\vec{u}\in\F_q^{d}$ and a parameter $k\in\N$, it must take $ N^{\delta k}$ time to distinguish the following two cases:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\yes$ Case: There exists $\vec x\in\F_q^{N}$ with $\|\vec x\|_0\le k$ such that $\vec H\vec x=\vec u$.
        \item $\no$ Case: For every $\vec x\in\F_q^{N}$ with $\|\vec x\|_0\le \gamma k$, we have $\vec H\vec x\neq\vec u$.
    \end{itemize}

