\label{thm:bqp-completeness}

  The following hold:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item The trace distance version of $(\alpha,\beta,l)$-\textsc{Pure Product State} is in $\cls{BQP}$ for all $l$ and all $\alpha<\beta\frac{\sqrt{11}}{32}$. (It is implicit here and throughout the rest of the paper that the gap between
  $\alpha$ and $\beta \frac{\sqrt{11}}{32}$ is larger than an inverse polynomial in the input length.)
  \item The one-way LOCC version of $(\varepsilon,2-\varepsilon)$-\textsc{Bipartite Pure Product State} is $\cls{BQP}$-hard, even when $\varepsilon$ decays exponentially in the input length.
  \end{enumerate}
  Thus, both problems are $\cls{BQP}$-complete for all $l\geq 2$ and all $(\alpha,\beta)$ with $0<\alpha<\beta\frac{\sqrt{11}}{32}$ and $\beta<2$.

